          "type": "turn",
          "question": "I can only get a few words out before I have to stop for air, and my rescue puffer isn't doing anything tonight.",
          "answer": "**Seek emergency care now. Call your local emergency number (for example 911 or 999, or 112 in Europe) and do not wait to ask more questions.**\n\nBeing able to say only a few words per breath, with a rescue inhaler that is not helping, is a sign of a severe breathing emergency. Our urgency tool returned \"routine\" because its fixed list did not match this exact wording. I am not following that tier, because the guidance says to escalate on the symptom itself. The \"Severe difficulty breathing\" guidance (red-flag-severe-breathing-difficulty) says that causes of this kind of breathlessness can be immediately life-threatening, and that they cannot be reliably told apart without assessment.\n\nWhile you wait for help:\n- Sit upright, which is usually the easiest position.\n- Loosen tight clothing and stay as calm as you can, since anxiety makes breathing harder.\n- Use your reliever inhaler as your own asthma action plan says. The same guidance notes that people with prescribed reliever or emergency medication use it per their plan while help is on the way.\n- If someone is with you, ask them to stay with you and to call for help if you cannot speak or become drowsy.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
